WEBVTT

00:00.550 --> 00:01.620
- [Instructor] In many other videos,

00:01.620 --> 00:03.350
we've taken equations like this

00:03.350 --> 00:07.480
and tried to quote solve for X.

00:07.480 --> 00:10.270
What we're gonna do in
this video is deepen

00:10.270 --> 00:12.970
our understanding a little
bit about what's going on

00:12.970 --> 00:15.130
and really think about
the notion of equivalence

00:15.130 --> 00:16.870
or equivalence statements.

00:16.870 --> 00:19.540
So let me write that down, equivalence.

00:19.540 --> 00:21.100
Now, what do I mean by equivalence?

00:21.100 --> 00:23.560
Well, I'll use this equation here

00:23.560 --> 00:27.310
to essentially keep rewriting
it in equivalent ways

00:27.310 --> 00:29.680
and I'll talk a little bit
more about what that means.

00:29.680 --> 00:31.870
So one thing that I
could do to essentially

00:31.870 --> 00:34.520
write the same equivalence statement

00:34.520 --> 00:39.030
is I could distribute
this three onto the X

00:39.030 --> 00:42.240
or onto the X plus one
and then this part of it

00:42.240 --> 00:46.580
could be rewritten as three X plus three

00:46.580 --> 00:50.240
and then we have minus X is equal to nine.

00:50.240 --> 00:54.000
Now, what I will say or what
it might be obvious to you

00:54.000 --> 00:56.570
on some level is this top equation

00:56.570 --> 00:59.980
and this second equation are equivalent.

00:59.980 --> 01:01.120
What does that mean?

01:01.120 --> 01:05.380
It means if one of them
is true for given X,

01:05.380 --> 01:08.790
the other one will be true
for that same X as well,

01:08.790 --> 01:12.430
and vice versa and we can write other

01:12.430 --> 01:13.970
equivalence statements.

01:13.970 --> 01:17.350
For example, if we were
to combine X terms,

01:17.350 --> 01:20.330
if we were to take three
X and then minus X,

01:20.330 --> 01:23.860
right over there, I could
rewrite that as two X

01:23.860 --> 01:28.860
and then I have plus
three is equal to nine.

01:29.490 --> 01:32.700
Now all three of these
statements are equivalent.

01:32.700 --> 01:35.070
If there's an X where two times that X

01:35.070 --> 01:36.720
plus three is equal to nine,

01:36.720 --> 01:40.570
then it is also the case that
three times that X plus one

01:40.570 --> 01:42.920
minus X is equal to nine and vice versa.

01:42.920 --> 01:45.840
If there's some X that would
make this top equation true

01:45.840 --> 01:48.730
then it's going to make
this last equation true.

01:48.730 --> 01:52.400
And we can do other equivalence
preserving operations

01:52.400 --> 01:53.770
and you've seen them before.

01:53.770 --> 01:56.130
You could subtract three from both sides.

01:56.130 --> 02:00.170
In general, if you are adding
or subtracting the same value

02:00.170 --> 02:03.520
from both sides, it is
equivalence preserving.

02:03.520 --> 02:05.680
If you're distributing a value like we did

02:05.680 --> 02:09.080
in that first step that
is equivalence preserving.

02:09.080 --> 02:12.500
If you combine like terms so to speak,

02:12.500 --> 02:14.000
that is equivalence preserving

02:14.000 --> 02:16.610
and so here we'll do an
equivalence preserving operation.

02:16.610 --> 02:18.800
We'll subtract three from both sides

02:18.800 --> 02:23.540
and you would get two X is equal to six

02:23.540 --> 02:26.370
and once again, any X that
satisfies this last equation

02:26.370 --> 02:29.530
will satisfy any of the other
equations and vice versa.

02:29.530 --> 02:31.810
Any X that's satisfies any
of these other equations

02:31.810 --> 02:33.060
will satisfy this last one

02:33.060 --> 02:35.970
and so they are all
equivalent to each other.

02:35.970 --> 02:38.700
And then another equivalence
preserving operation

02:38.700 --> 02:43.700
is to multiply or divide both
sides by a non zero constant.

02:44.960 --> 02:49.150
And so here we could
divide both sides by two,

02:49.150 --> 02:52.040
two is not zero and it's constant

02:52.040 --> 02:55.230
and if we did that, we will get
another equivalent statement

02:55.230 --> 02:59.380
that X is equal to three.

02:59.380 --> 03:03.350
So any X that satisfies this
and there's one, X equals three

03:03.350 --> 03:05.910
would satisfy the other ones
and any X that satisfies

03:05.910 --> 03:07.970
any of the other ones
would satisfy this last one

03:07.970 --> 03:10.100
so these are all equivalent.

03:10.100 --> 03:11.730
So one way to think about it,

03:11.730 --> 03:15.580
adding the same number to
both sides of an equation

03:15.580 --> 03:18.150
that is equivalence preserving.

03:18.150 --> 03:23.030
Multiply or dividing both sides
by a non-zero constant value

03:23.030 --> 03:25.230
is equivalence preserving.

03:25.230 --> 03:27.310
Distributing like we
did in this first step

03:27.310 --> 03:29.410
equivalence preserving,
combining like terms

03:29.410 --> 03:31.140
equivalence preserving.

03:31.140 --> 03:32.010
Now you're probably saying,

03:32.010 --> 03:36.200
well, what are some non
equivalence preserving operations?

03:36.200 --> 03:37.970
Well, imagine something like this,

03:37.970 --> 03:39.730
lemme just start with
something very obvious.

03:39.730 --> 03:41.950
If I said that X is equal to two

03:41.950 --> 03:44.240
a non equivalence preserving operation

03:44.240 --> 03:48.340
is if I were to add or
subtract or multiply or divide

03:48.340 --> 03:51.410
only one side of this equation by a value

03:51.410 --> 03:53.860
let's say I only added
one to the left side.

03:53.860 --> 03:56.710
Then I would have X
plus one is equal to two

03:56.710 --> 03:59.593
and it is not the case that
anything that satisfies

03:59.593 --> 04:03.080
this second equation
satisfies the top equation

04:03.080 --> 04:04.860
or vice versa.

04:04.860 --> 04:07.660
X equals two clearly
satisfies the top equation

04:07.660 --> 04:09.140
but it doesn't satisfy the second one,

04:09.140 --> 04:11.310
that's because we did a non equivalence

04:11.310 --> 04:13.040
preserving operation.

04:13.040 --> 04:17.040
Likewise, if I only multiplied
the right hand side by three,

04:17.040 --> 04:19.980
I would get X is equal to six.

04:19.980 --> 04:23.660
Well, by only multiplying
the right hand side by value

04:23.660 --> 04:27.220
it's not the case that anything
that satisfies X equals six

04:27.220 --> 04:31.260
will satisfy X equals two,
that is somewhat obvious here.

04:31.260 --> 04:33.890
Now there's a little
bit trickier scenarios,

04:33.890 --> 04:38.890
let's say we have the equation
five X is equal to six X.

04:43.060 --> 04:47.510
Now, one temptation is, well,
I wanna do the same thing

04:47.510 --> 04:52.510
to both sides, I could just
divide both sides by X,

04:52.510 --> 04:54.580
what'll happen in that scenario?

04:54.580 --> 04:57.870
Well, if you divide both sides by X

04:57.870 --> 04:59.870
you could think that an
equivalence statement

04:59.870 --> 05:04.030
is that five is equal to six
and you know that there's no X

05:04.030 --> 05:05.710
for which five is equal to six.

05:05.710 --> 05:09.270
You can't make five equal
six or six equal five

05:09.270 --> 05:11.670
and so this would somehow
make you imply that okay,

05:11.670 --> 05:13.160
there's no X that can satisfy.

05:13.160 --> 05:15.460
If you assume that these
were equivalence statements,

05:15.460 --> 05:18.000
you'd say there's nothing that
could satisfy five equal six

05:18.000 --> 05:22.320
so maybe there's nothing that
satisfies this top equation.

05:22.320 --> 05:25.730
But this actually isn't an
equivalence preserving operation

05:25.730 --> 05:28.250
because you're actually
dealing with a scenario

05:28.250 --> 05:32.870
where X is equal to zero
and you're dividing by zero.

05:32.870 --> 05:34.780
And so you have to be very careful

05:34.780 --> 05:37.280
when you're dividing by a variable,

05:37.280 --> 05:40.400
especially if the variable
that makes that equation true

05:40.400 --> 05:42.350
happens to be zero.

05:42.350 --> 05:45.200
And so in order to be clear

05:45.200 --> 05:47.470
that you're preserving equivalence here,

05:47.470 --> 05:49.400
the way that I would tackle it is

05:49.400 --> 05:53.050
I would subtract five X from both sides

05:53.050 --> 05:54.980
and if you do that, and
that is an equivalence

05:54.980 --> 05:58.980
preserving operation, you
could subtract that expression

05:58.980 --> 06:01.230
from both sides or that
term from both sides

06:01.230 --> 06:05.410
and then you would be left
with zero is equal to X

06:05.410 --> 06:10.410
and now zero equals X
and five X equals six X,

06:10.430 --> 06:12.460
these are equivalence statements.

06:12.460 --> 06:14.250
They are equivalent equations

06:14.250 --> 06:16.800
anything that makes this
one true is going to make

06:16.800 --> 06:18.880
that one true and anyone
that makes that one true

06:18.880 --> 06:21.260
is going to make this one true.

06:21.260 --> 06:24.270
Now one last thing you
might have heard me say,

06:24.270 --> 06:28.700
you can multiply or
divide by non-zero value

06:28.700 --> 06:30.800
and that's going to be
equivalence preserving.

06:30.800 --> 06:32.170
And hopefully you've just got a sense

06:32.170 --> 06:33.960
of why dividing by zero
is not a good idea.

06:33.960 --> 06:36.170
In fact, dividing by zero
is always going to be

06:36.170 --> 06:38.860
a strange thing and it's undefined,

06:38.860 --> 06:41.070
but also multiplying by zero.

06:41.070 --> 06:44.560
For example, if I had, let's say, actually

06:44.560 --> 06:45.393
lemme start over here.

06:45.393 --> 06:49.630
If I had two X is equal to
six, and if I were to multiply

06:49.630 --> 06:54.630
both sides by zero, I would
get zero is equal to zero

06:54.960 --> 06:59.320
and zero equals zero is true for any X.

06:59.320 --> 07:03.670
Zero is always going to be equal to zero

07:03.670 --> 07:06.180
but the problem is, is that
first statement isn't true

07:06.180 --> 07:09.200
for all X it's only
true for X equals three.

07:09.200 --> 07:12.870
So these two are not
equivalent statements.

07:12.870 --> 07:17.360
They have a different set
of Xs that will satisfy them

07:17.360 --> 07:19.460
and so you have to be very
careful when you're dealing

07:19.460 --> 07:21.680
with things that are either are zero

07:21.680 --> 07:22.920
you can add or subtract zeros,

07:22.920 --> 07:25.930
obviously that's not
gonna change things much,

07:25.930 --> 07:27.920
but when you multiply both sides by zero,

07:27.920 --> 07:29.240
you can start getting things

07:29.240 --> 07:30.930
that are not equivalence statements

07:30.930 --> 07:33.030
and when you multiply or divide by things

07:33.030 --> 07:35.890
that could be zero, like variables,

07:35.890 --> 07:38.593
that also is a dangerous game to play.
